// What a workload is, as this cloud knows it.
//
// # There is no grant, and that is the whole argument for it
//
// Every other package here is authority over something: a store, a queue, a
// bucket, a secret. This is authority over nothing — it answers what the
// platform already put in the instance's own environment and headers, about the
// instance itself. A workload cannot reach anything with it that it could not
// reach without it.
//
// So it is unconditional, and it is the ONE capability in this tree that is.
// The ambient-authority rule this cloud holds — a call that names no resource is
// declared or it is denied — is about reach, and there is none here to declare.
//
// # Why it exists rather than a pile of environment variables
//
// It is a pile of environment variables today, effectively, and that is the
// problem: every author invents their own names for the same six facts, every
// framework's convention differs, and a library that wants to tag a metric with
// the environment it is running in cannot know what this deployment called it.
// One call with one shape means an SDK can log, trace and shard without the
// author wiring anything.
//
// # What it does not answer
//
// ANYTHING ABOUT THE REQUEST. Which invocation this is, who called, what the
// trace id is — those belong to a call and not to the instance, and an instance
// serving several at once could only answer for one of them. They travel in the
// request's own headers.
//
// ANYTHING ABOUT ANOTHER WORKLOAD. This is the workload asking about itself.
//
// # What `node` gives away, and why it is here anyway
//
// Guest code is an untrusted actor in this cloud, so every field here was asked
// the same question: what does knowing it buy an attacker. `project`,
// `environment`, `kind`, `name` and `scope` are the guest's own configuration,
// which it was deployed with. `revision` and `artifact` name the guest ITSELF —
// learning your own content id tells you what you already are.
//
// `node` is the only one that names something outside the workload, and it is
// the peer's PUBLIC swarm identity: on an open swarm anybody dialling it
// already sees it, and on a private one a guest cannot reach the swarm at all
// — it gets no socket. What it buys a tenant is the ability to notice that two
// of its own instances landed on one machine, which every cloud tells you
// (AWS's instance-id, Kubernetes' node name) because scheduling-aware code
// needs it.
//
// # It is not called `self`
//
// v1's host module was, and `self` is a keyword in Rust — wit-bindgen cannot
// generate a module for a package named that and panics rather than escaping
// it. `workload` is the vocabulary's own word for the thing anyway, which makes
// this the same kind of rename `channel` was.
package taubyte:workload@0.1.0;

interface identity {
    // Where this instance came from and what it is.
    record identity {
        // The project and the environment it is deployed in — `shop`, `prod`.
        project: string,
        environment: string,

        // What the vocabulary calls this workload, and what its author named
        // it: `function`, `checkout`.
        kind: string,
        name: string,

        // The namespaces it lives in, outermost first. Empty at the project
        // root.
        scope: list<string>,

        // The configuration revision this was deployed from. It changes on
        // every deploy and on nothing else, which is what makes it the thing to
        // tag a log line or a cache key with.
        revision: string,

        // The build artifact this instance is running. "" for a workload the
        // platform itself runs rather than one that was built.
        artifact: string,

        // The peer this instance is running on. "" when the tier was composed
        // without a node identity, which is what a test does.
        node: string,
    }

    get: func() -> identity;
}

world imports {
    import identity;
}
